wasm jit: a reused load is widened into an i64 register; invalid modules are final (gex)

On wasm64 a register written by both a 32-bit load and a negative ICONST is an i64 local (the
constant must stay sign-extended). The load-CSE hit path copied the earlier load's local into it
with a plain local.get/local.set, so `x == 65535 ? -1 : x` over a field loaded twice
(AssemblyName.FillName) emitted a module that does not validate ("type mismatch: expected i64,
found i32"). The hit path now goes through wasm_ld_as/wasm_st_from at the load's own valtype, as
the miss path does.

The instantiate error buffer grows from 192 to 1024 bytes so the validation message survives, and
wasmtime's "validating the module" counts as permanent like V8's CompileError: emission is
deterministic, so a retry rebuilds the same bytes. In the embed suite FillName had been
re-emitted 106 times. Hollow Knight's hg17 reported invalid=1322.

Test: T.LcseWide (VerKey is invalid before, compiles after), checked against CoreCLR.

